1
00:00:02,780 --> 00:00:05,120
Hello everyone and welcome back to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:05,360 --> 00:00:06,480
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:06,480 --> 00:00:11,060
My name is Catherine and today we have an upper intermediate level lesson for all of you.

4
00:00:11,840 --> 00:00:12,840
That's right.

5
00:00:12,840 --> 00:00:18,440
We're going to be talking business on this occasion on EnglishPod but specifically about planning a business.

6
00:00:19,060 --> 00:00:19,420
Right.

7
00:00:19,440 --> 00:00:23,260
So before a business opens, a lot of planning has to happen.

8
00:00:23,420 --> 00:00:29,980
That means people will decide what kind of a product they want to sell, what kind of a market they want to sell to, etc.

9
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:32,520
So that is the focus of today's lesson.

10
00:00:32,520 --> 00:00:37,240
Let's take a listen to the dialogue for the first time and we'll be back in a minute to talk about what's going on.

11
00:00:46,760 --> 00:00:47,580
I've had it.

12
00:00:47,940 --> 00:00:50,440
I'm done working for a company that is taking me nowhere.

13
00:00:50,980 --> 00:00:51,880
So what are you going to do?

14
00:00:52,320 --> 00:00:53,400
Just quit?

15
00:00:53,400 --> 00:00:55,060
That's exactly what I'm going to do.

16
00:00:55,840 --> 00:00:57,420
I've decided to create my own company.

17
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:01,740
I'm going to write up a business plan, get some investors and start working for myself.

18
00:01:02,320 --> 00:01:04,400
Have you ever written up a business plan before?

19
00:01:04,900 --> 00:01:05,880
Well, no.

20
00:01:06,370 --> 00:01:07,270
It can't be that hard.

21
00:01:07,730 --> 00:01:09,950
I mean, all you have to do is explain your business.

22
00:01:10,770 --> 00:01:11,950
How are you going to do things?

23
00:01:12,270 --> 00:01:13,030
And that's it, right?

24
00:01:13,720 --> 00:01:14,770
You couldn't be more wrong.

25
00:01:15,130 --> 00:01:20,630
A well-written business plan will include an executive summary which highlights the idea of the business in two pages or less.

26
00:01:21,310 --> 00:01:26,570
Then you need to describe your company with information such as what type of legal structure it has, history, etc.

27
00:01:27,070 --> 00:01:28,550
Well that seems easy enough.

28
00:01:29,030 --> 00:01:29,630
Wait, there's more!

29
00:01:30,270 --> 00:01:35,210
Then you need to introduce and describe your goods or services, what they are and how they're different from competitors.

30
00:01:35,810 --> 00:01:38,030
Then comes the hard part, a market analysis.

31
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:42,380
You need to investigate and analyse hundreds of variables.

32
00:01:42,380 --> 00:01:48,850
You need to take into consideration socio-economic factors from GDP per capita to how many children on average the population has.

33
00:01:49,680 --> 00:01:57,530
All this information is useful so that you can move on to your strategy and implementation stage where you will describe in detail how you will actually execute your idea.

34
00:01:58,210 --> 00:01:59,310
Geez, is that all?

35
00:01:59,710 --> 00:02:00,090
Almost.

36
00:02:00,650 --> 00:02:04,450
The most important piece of information for your investors will be the financial analysis.

37
00:02:05,410 --> 00:02:08,170
Here you will calculate and estimate sales, cash flow and profits.

38
00:02:08,890 --> 00:02:12,330
After all, people will want to know when they will begin to see a return on their investment.

39
00:02:14,620 --> 00:02:19,760
I think I'll just stick to my old job and save myself all the hassle of trying to start up a business.

40
00:02:25,720 --> 00:02:26,720
Alright we're back.

41
00:02:26,720 --> 00:02:33,600
So the guy wanted to open up a business but his friend explained that it's not so easy and gave a lot of interesting words and phrases there.

42
00:02:33,600 --> 00:02:33,860
He did.

43
00:02:34,600 --> 00:02:35,080
He seemed a little naive.

44
00:02:35,600 --> 00:02:40,160
Like he didn't really know how much work is involved in opening a business.

45
00:02:40,160 --> 00:02:41,160
Exactly.

46
00:02:41,160 --> 00:02:44,560
So I think we should take a look at some of the words used on Language Takeaway.

47
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:47,390
Language Takeaway

48
00:02:49,840 --> 00:02:54,660
The first word is the heart and soul of the process before you open a business.

49
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:56,140
It's the business plan.

50
00:02:56,480 --> 00:02:58,600
Alright so the business plan.

51
00:02:58,600 --> 00:03:04,700
As you said it's a noun and it's basically a document that explains what's going on in the business.

52
00:03:05,360 --> 00:03:06,360
Exactly.

53
00:03:06,360 --> 00:03:10,930
It's a paper that you write about the business that you want to open and it's a plan.

54
00:03:11,320 --> 00:03:12,490
So that's what the name says.

55
00:03:12,690 --> 00:03:18,190
You have to tell what you will do, how much money you will spend, how much money you will need.

56
00:03:19,570 --> 00:03:25,770
Because if you're asking people for money, they want to know where the money will be spent if it's going to be spent on wise decisions.

57
00:03:26,290 --> 00:03:26,730
Exactly.

58
00:03:27,640 --> 00:03:33,560
And as you mentioned, you show this business plan maybe to a bank but also maybe to an investor.

59
00:03:33,590 --> 00:03:34,070
That's right.

60
00:03:34,250 --> 00:03:35,490
So investor is our next word.

61
00:03:35,650 --> 00:03:37,750
It's another noun but this noun is a person.

62
00:03:38,450 --> 00:03:40,090
A person is an investor.

63
00:03:40,090 --> 00:03:45,790
If he or she gives money to someone else and expects more money to come back.

64
00:03:46,210 --> 00:03:46,450
Right.

65
00:03:46,910 --> 00:03:55,390
So a person that's starting out a business maybe has enough money himself to open up a business but sometimes you need extra money so you look for investors.

66
00:03:55,850 --> 00:03:57,510
People who are interested in giving you money.

67
00:03:57,830 --> 00:03:59,990
For example, I have a cupcake company.

68
00:04:00,290 --> 00:04:04,430
I want to sell cupcakes to people and I need a thousand dollars.

69
00:04:04,650 --> 00:04:05,850
So Marco, do you want to be an investor?

70
00:04:06,080 --> 00:04:07,080
Do you want to invest some money?

71
00:04:07,080 --> 00:04:08,080
Yes.

72
00:04:08,080 --> 00:04:09,080
For sure.

73
00:04:09,080 --> 00:04:10,080
A cupcake company.

74
00:04:10,080 --> 00:04:11,080
That sounds great.

75
00:04:11,080 --> 00:04:12,690
I know but what happens if I don't sell cupcakes?

76
00:04:13,230 --> 00:04:16,550
That's a problem for you because you have made a risky investment.

77
00:04:17,030 --> 00:04:17,510
That's right.

78
00:04:17,790 --> 00:04:19,770
So that's what happens with investors.

79
00:04:20,430 --> 00:04:29,790
But now if you want to attract the attention of your investors with your business plan, I think part of the most important aspects of this plan is the executive summary.

80
00:04:30,360 --> 00:04:36,140
A summary is usually an outline of the important points of a paper or a document.

81
00:04:36,800 --> 00:04:46,630
So in this case I could say, Catherine's cupcake company will sell delicious cupcakes of 10 different flavors to the market of 5 year olds, 55 year olds.

82
00:04:47,320 --> 00:04:47,850
People will love them.

83
00:04:48,320 --> 00:04:53,150
She has a great secret recipe and she plans to triple her investments by next year.

84
00:04:53,600 --> 00:04:54,600
Right.

85
00:04:54,600 --> 00:04:55,600
That's exactly it.

86
00:04:55,600 --> 00:05:03,570
So if an investor reads the executive summary, which shouldn't be more than two pages long, you can pretty much get the idea of what the business is about.

87
00:05:04,280 --> 00:05:06,880
So if you want more details, you have to read the rest of the business plan.

88
00:05:07,280 --> 00:05:10,780
But mainly with the executive summary, you can more or less know what it's all about.

89
00:05:11,240 --> 00:05:11,420
Exactly.

90
00:05:12,240 --> 00:05:19,120
So a summary, like I said, is something that should be short and it should give you all of the most important information about something.

91
00:05:19,120 --> 00:05:20,120
Exactly.

92
00:05:20,120 --> 00:05:29,400
And while they were talking more in depth about analyzing things and doing some research, and among those things, the girl mentioned the socio-economic factors.

93
00:05:30,010 --> 00:05:41,870
Okay, when I said we're going to sell cupcakes from my company to people between the ages of 5 and 55, we need to analyze, we need to look at their socio-economic factors.

94
00:05:42,880 --> 00:05:43,910
Socio comes from the word social.

95
00:05:44,320 --> 00:05:49,440
It means where in society they are, they're upper class, middle class or lower class.

96
00:05:49,440 --> 00:05:50,950
Economic means how much money do they make?

97
00:05:51,080 --> 00:05:54,340
Do they make $10,000 a year or $100,000 a year?

98
00:05:54,720 --> 00:06:03,640
And so the kind of people we want to sell my cupcakes to will change according to their socio-economic factors.

99
00:06:03,640 --> 00:06:03,880
That's right.

100
00:06:04,640 --> 00:06:07,680
So as you mentioned, socio comes from the word social or from society.

101
00:06:08,100 --> 00:06:10,580
So you're analyzing these factors from society.

102
00:06:11,800 --> 00:06:16,600
And well, part of these factors is the GDP per capita.

103
00:06:16,600 --> 00:06:17,780
So we have two different words there.

104
00:06:17,960 --> 00:06:19,040
An acronym, GDP.

105
00:06:20,160 --> 00:06:24,280
GDP, gross domestic product.

106
00:06:24,280 --> 00:06:27,910
That might seem confusing because gross means different things at different times.

107
00:06:28,600 --> 00:06:31,640
But in this case, domestic means our country.

108
00:06:31,640 --> 00:06:35,230
So within a country, all of the products added up together.

109
00:06:35,710 --> 00:06:35,870
Right.

110
00:06:36,490 --> 00:06:37,970
And gross is basically that.

111
00:06:38,390 --> 00:06:42,890
Just all the products added up together without taking into consideration any other things.

112
00:06:43,490 --> 00:06:48,170
So the GDP is basically all the goods and services produced in a country within a given time.

113
00:06:49,240 --> 00:06:50,430
And what about per capita though?

114
00:06:51,240 --> 00:06:52,630
So per capita comes from Latin.

115
00:06:53,320 --> 00:06:55,680
Per means four or each.

116
00:06:55,680 --> 00:06:56,920
And capita is a head.

117
00:06:56,920 --> 00:06:59,040
So this is per person.

118
00:06:59,040 --> 00:07:06,310
So GDP per capita means how much, how many goods and services are made divided by the number of people in that place.

119
00:07:06,910 --> 00:07:07,590
Exactly.

120
00:07:08,160 --> 00:07:14,240
So you probably have these indicators in your country or your own language as well.

121
00:07:14,240 --> 00:07:15,360
But that's basically it.

122
00:07:15,360 --> 00:07:17,750
And the acronym is gross domestic product.

123
00:07:18,760 --> 00:07:27,700
And just to explain a little bit, if you see this in Wikipedia or in an encyclopedia, normally a very low GDP per capita is $500, American dollars.

124
00:07:27,720 --> 00:07:29,800
And a high one would be $35,000.

125
00:07:29,800 --> 00:07:31,080
$35,000, yeah.

126
00:07:31,080 --> 00:07:32,600
And that's usually per year, right?

127
00:07:32,600 --> 00:07:35,740
Because that's usually how it's taken into consideration.

128
00:07:36,240 --> 00:07:37,140
The time is a year.

129
00:07:37,960 --> 00:07:38,200
All right.

130
00:07:38,800 --> 00:07:42,320
So those are the words that we have for you on language takeaway.

131
00:07:42,320 --> 00:07:45,660
Now why don't we move on to a couple of different phrases on Fluency Builder.

132
00:07:48,260 --> 00:07:49,080
Fluency Builder.

133
00:07:49,840 --> 00:07:50,840
Okay.

134
00:07:51,680 --> 00:07:52,480
So this is important.

135
00:07:53,160 --> 00:07:56,560
First phrase, Marco, has to do with our last first phrase.

136
00:07:56,560 --> 00:07:57,260
We had a business plan.

137
00:07:57,560 --> 00:07:57,960
So what do you do?

138
00:07:58,560 --> 00:07:59,640
You write up a business plan.

139
00:07:59,920 --> 00:08:00,060
Right.

140
00:08:00,200 --> 00:08:05,380
And that's a common way of saying to write or to create, to work on a business plan, to write up.

141
00:08:05,840 --> 00:08:06,120
Right.

142
00:08:06,120 --> 00:08:09,620
So it's something that we say that's very colloquial.

143
00:08:09,680 --> 00:08:11,240
Something that is very spoken.

144
00:08:11,780 --> 00:08:17,180
And I can't explain why we say it, but you'll sound very fluent if you say, I'm going to write up a business plan.

145
00:08:17,320 --> 00:08:18,980
Or why don't you write up a business plan?

146
00:08:19,120 --> 00:08:19,240
Right.

147
00:08:20,080 --> 00:08:25,440
So it would be correct to say, I'm going to write a business plan or I have to write a business plan.

148
00:08:25,440 --> 00:08:30,240
But if you use this phrase, I'm going to write up a business plan, it's more fluent, right?

149
00:08:30,440 --> 00:08:31,440
It's just more native-like.

150
00:08:31,480 --> 00:08:31,840
Exactly.

151
00:08:32,440 --> 00:08:32,880
All right.

152
00:08:33,220 --> 00:08:36,320
And our next phrase is a return on investment.

153
00:08:37,080 --> 00:08:37,300
Okay.

154
00:08:37,720 --> 00:08:39,760
So early we talked about investors.

155
00:08:39,760 --> 00:08:43,070
The money that they invest is called an investment.

156
00:08:43,280 --> 00:08:44,280
Okay.

157
00:08:44,280 --> 00:08:46,320
These words are all very closely related.

158
00:08:46,320 --> 00:08:58,840
And when Marco decided to give money to my cupcake business, he expects the money to come back and he expects more actually to come back than what he put in.

159
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:00,760
So that's called a return on investment.

160
00:09:01,040 --> 00:09:02,020
The money that's the difference.

161
00:09:02,640 --> 00:09:07,440
So if he gives me a thousand dollars and I give him back $1,500 in a year, he made $500.

162
00:09:08,180 --> 00:09:08,300
That's amazing.

163
00:09:08,840 --> 00:09:10,920
So that means it's a good return on his investment.

164
00:09:10,920 --> 00:09:11,920
Right.

165
00:09:11,920 --> 00:09:14,680
So that's the term that we use, a return on investment.

166
00:09:16,080 --> 00:09:19,680
So you don't say to return on investment, you see a return on investment.

167
00:09:19,880 --> 00:09:20,000
Exactly.

168
00:09:20,220 --> 00:09:22,420
It's a thing, it's a number, it's a quantity.

169
00:09:22,940 --> 00:09:23,820
It's something you can count.

170
00:09:24,080 --> 00:09:24,420
Right.

171
00:09:25,200 --> 00:09:30,560
And well, in the end, after seeing that this business plan is not so easy, the guy said, you know what?

172
00:09:30,900 --> 00:09:34,120
I'm not going to quit my job and just save myself the hassle.

173
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:36,000
What's a hassle?

174
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:36,400
The hassle.

175
00:09:36,700 --> 00:09:37,920
That's an interesting word.

176
00:09:38,440 --> 00:09:45,920
So if I'm going to save myself the hassle, a hassle is like the problem or the hard work that it involves.

177
00:09:45,920 --> 00:09:46,670
It's a lot of trouble.

178
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:47,570
A lot of trouble.

179
00:09:48,090 --> 00:09:55,390
So I was going to cook some dinner tonight, but I'm going to save myself the hassle of going to the grocery store and buying it and cooking it by just ordering food.

180
00:09:55,730 --> 00:09:55,930
Right.

181
00:09:56,350 --> 00:09:57,210
So that's a good example.

182
00:09:57,470 --> 00:10:03,690
Instead of doing all the work of cooking, you just save yourself the hassle, pick up the phone and get food delivered to your house.

183
00:10:03,910 --> 00:10:04,030
Right.

184
00:10:04,920 --> 00:10:08,880
So you can change here, you can say save myself, save yourself, save his self.

185
00:10:08,880 --> 00:10:10,440
So you change that pronoun.

186
00:10:10,440 --> 00:10:11,440
The pronoun.

187
00:10:11,440 --> 00:10:12,440
Exactly.

188
00:10:12,440 --> 00:10:15,410
So you can save yourself the hassle or I'm just going to save myself the hassle.

189
00:10:16,010 --> 00:10:16,230
Okay.

190
00:10:16,510 --> 00:10:18,450
So that's about it for fluency builder.

191
00:10:18,710 --> 00:10:22,870
Let's listen to today's dialogue one more time and we'll be back in a minute to talk about business.

192
00:10:32,640 --> 00:10:33,400
I've had it.

193
00:10:33,900 --> 00:10:36,280
I'm done working for a company that is taking me nowhere.

194
00:10:36,960 --> 00:10:38,160
So what are you going to do?

195
00:10:38,160 --> 00:10:39,280
Just quit?

196
00:10:39,280 --> 00:10:40,900
That's exactly what I'm going to do.

197
00:10:41,640 --> 00:10:43,240
I've decided to create my own company.

198
00:10:43,800 --> 00:10:47,600
I'm going to write up a business plan, get some investors and start working for myself.

199
00:10:48,140 --> 00:10:50,220
Have you ever written up a business plan before?

200
00:10:50,680 --> 00:10:53,100
Well, no, it can't be that hard.

201
00:10:53,520 --> 00:10:55,780
I mean, all you have to do is explain your business.

202
00:10:56,580 --> 00:10:57,800
How are you going to do things?

203
00:10:58,320 --> 00:10:58,860
And that's it, right?

204
00:10:59,600 --> 00:11:00,580
You couldn't be more wrong.

205
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:06,460
A well-written business plan will include an executive summary, which highlights the idea of the business in two pages or less.

206
00:11:07,280 --> 00:11:12,400
Then you need to describe your company with information such as what type of legal structure it has, history, etc.

207
00:11:12,960 --> 00:11:14,380
Well, that seems easy enough.

208
00:11:14,780 --> 00:11:15,440
Wait, there's more.

209
00:11:15,980 --> 00:11:21,000
Then you need to introduce and describe your goods or services, what they are and how they're different from competitors.

210
00:11:21,960 --> 00:11:23,800
Then comes the hard part, a market analysis.

211
00:11:24,820 --> 00:11:27,200
You need to investigate and analyze hundreds of variables.

212
00:11:28,120 --> 00:11:34,660
You need to take into consideration socioeconomic factors from GDP per capita to how many children on average the population has.

213
00:11:35,130 --> 00:11:43,350
All this information is useful so that you can move on to your strategy and implementation stage, where you will describe in detail how you will actually execute your idea.

214
00:11:43,990 --> 00:11:45,110
Geez, is that all?

215
00:11:45,530 --> 00:11:45,890
Almost.

216
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:51,320
The most important piece of information for your investors will be the financial analysis.

217
00:11:51,320 --> 00:11:53,990
Here you will calculate and estimate sales, cash flow and profits.

218
00:11:54,960 --> 00:11:58,170
After all, people will want to know when they will begin to see a return on their investment.

219
00:12:00,450 --> 00:12:05,590
I think I'll just stick to my old job and save myself all the hassle of trying to start up a business.

220
00:12:15,340 --> 00:12:19,380
So it was pretty interesting that he wanted to quit his job and just, you know, set up a business.

221
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:23,040
But actually, many people say that business plans are unnecessary.

222
00:12:23,340 --> 00:12:23,660
Really?

223
00:12:24,180 --> 00:12:26,640
Do these people have lots of money and they don't need investors?

224
00:12:27,360 --> 00:12:35,940
I think the problem is that there are many books out there that give you clear examples of companies, Fortune 500 companies, that started out without a business plan.

225
00:12:35,940 --> 00:12:38,800
So I think many people take this as a rule.

226
00:12:38,900 --> 00:12:39,640
A rule.

227
00:12:39,800 --> 00:12:44,400
Like, well, they didn't need one or it's not really necessary or why should I write up a business plan?

228
00:12:44,400 --> 00:12:53,360
Well, I'm not an entrepreneur and I'm not a business person, but my feeling is that a business plan is as much for investors as it is for yourself.

229
00:12:53,960 --> 00:12:56,000
It helps you clarify your goals.

230
00:12:56,520 --> 00:13:02,840
It helps you explain your objectives to your future colleagues.

231
00:13:02,840 --> 00:13:09,160
And I think it's just a really good way to map out the first year of a business because it tends to, a lot of businesses fail in that first year.

232
00:13:09,160 --> 00:13:10,670
So I think this is a really important step.

233
00:13:10,970 --> 00:13:11,410
Yeah, exactly.

234
00:13:12,010 --> 00:13:12,890
So it's very interesting.

235
00:13:13,190 --> 00:13:22,010
We've talked a little bit about business here, but we would like to know your ideas or your comments or any other suggestions that you have about this topic or any topic on EnglishPod.

236
00:13:22,410 --> 00:13:25,330
So why don't you send us a comment or a personal message.

237
00:13:25,800 --> 00:13:28,640
We are at EnglishPod.com and we'll see you guys there.

238
00:13:28,710 --> 00:13:29,150
